(* ============================================================================
   21. TRAILING LINE COMMENTS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-041] (governs `inline_comment` in PART 3).
      - A `%%` token is a trailing comment marker only when the character
        immediately before it is a space or it starts the
        inline run (its first content character after leading whitespace).
      - AN INLINE RUN IS A DELIMITED REGION PARSED AS `inline_content` IN ITS
        OWN RIGHT: a paragraph, a heading's text, a definition term, a table
        cell, a figure caption, an admonition header, a container, link, span
        or fence label. A bracket run that stays literal, an inline note's body
        and an extension's content also hold a run, so a `%%` in one ends at its
        closing `]`: `^[%% c] d` is an empty note and ` d`. An unresolved
        reference is its literal source, `%%` included: `[a %% c][b] d` stays
        `[a %% c][b] d`, while `[a %% c] d` renders `[a] d`.
        Nothing else holds a run -- not markup INSIDE one, so
        an emphasis span's first content character does not start one; not a
        slot the grammar declares literal (`alt_text`, a code fence's
        `quoted_title`); and
        not a label matched verbatim as a key (a reference or footnote label).
        A paragraph's run starts at a line start, where PART 2's `%%` line
        reaches the case first.
      - When recognized, the `%%`, the whole run of spaces separating it
        from the text before it, and ALL remaining characters to the END OF THE
        RUN are consumed and produce no output. The separating run goes in
        full. A tab is content: `a⇥ %% c` renders `a⇥` (⇥ is a tab).
      - An escaped space (`\ `) is a space before the marker too: `x\ %% c`
        renders `x&nbsp;`. The escape keeps its no-break space; it is not part
        of the consumed separating run (carve#4274).
      - A RUN'S OWN CLOSER ENDS IT, and the line break ends a run that has
        none: `[a %% c](/u) t` renders `<p><a href="/u">a</a> t</p>`.
      - `%%` is NEVER recognized inside a code span or raw inline (PART 9 §20);
        those contexts pass both `%` characters through verbatim.
      - `\%%` (escaped first percent) is literal -- `%%` is literal text, not a
        comment marker.
      - Without a preceding space (e.g. `50%%` or `a%%b`) the `%%` is
        literal -- percentages and doubled-percent tokens in prose are safe.
      - The comment does NOT cross a line break; a soft-wrapped continuation on
        the next line of the same paragraph is unaffected (corpus 26-comments-6).

   21a. DELIMITED INLINE COMMENTS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-042] (governs `braced_comment` in
      PART 3). `%%` runs to the end of its inline RUN, so mid-line commenting
      already works wherever the structure supplies a boundary: a table cell
      ends at `|`, link text at `]`, and ANY CONSTRUCT WITH AN EXPLICIT CLOSER
      ends at that closer -- a forced span `{X...X}` at its `X}`, the combined
      `/*...*/` token at its `*/`, and the rest of the braced family the same
      way. In an editorial `{~...~>...~}` frame, a `%%` in either side runs
      to the frame's first raw `~}`; a `~>` after it is comment text (see the
      `{~` paragraph below). Plain prose supplies none, and this is the form for it:
      `foo {% bar %} baz` renders `<p>foo  baz</p>`.
      - Only the braced comment's own characters are removed. Spaces before
        and after it stay in the inline run, including a trailing space in
        `x {%%}`, which renders `<p>x </p>`.

      In a `{~` frame, the first raw `~}` ends a `%%` comment even when its
      body contains `~>`. That arrow is comment text and cannot split a
      substitution. If it was the only top-level arrow, the frame is
      unclosed: `{~a %% b~>c~} d` renders `<p>{~a d</p>`.
      - A paragraph holding only braced comments renders empty and takes
        pending attributes: `{% c %}` gives `<p></p>` and `- {% c %}` an empty
        `<li>`.
      - SPELLING. `{%` opens, the FIRST `%}` closes. One character per meaning,
        like the rest of the braced family ({^x^}, {,x,}, {+x+}, {-x-}), and the
        same spelling Djot uses, so a Djot document's comments keep working.
      - NO NESTING. A `{%` inside the content is ordinary comment text; the run
        ends at the first `%}`.
      - A WHOLE OPENER (CARVE-P3-025). `{%` followed by a grapheme_extender
        opens nothing and is text, like every braced opener.
      - UNTERMINATED STAYS LITERAL. `a {% oops` renders the braces (matching
        Djot, and the same visible-failure principle as `- {.notattr} Milk`).
      - VERBATIM CONTEXTS ARE OPAQUE, as for `%%` (§21): a code span or raw
        inline passes `{%` through.
      - TRANSPARENT TO INLINE STRUCTURE. A comment inside an emphasis run does
        not break it: `*bo{% c %}ld*` is one `<strong>`.
      - NO ATTRIBUTE ATTACHMENT. The comment takes no inline attribute slot
        and never joins an attribute run (CARVE-P3-017). A following inline
        attribute block is text: `*x*{% c %}{.k}` renders
        `<strong>x</strong>{.k}`; the block attaches to neither comment nor
        emphasis.
      - MULTI-LINE WITHIN A PARAGRAPH. The run may cross soft line breaks:
        `a {% one\ntwo %} b` renders `<p>a  b</p>`. It never joins two
        paragraphs across a blank line -- `%%%` is the block form (§28).
      - ESCAPING. `\{%` is literal text.
      - EVERY RENDER TARGET DROPS IT. HTML, Markdown, plain text and ANSI emit
        nothing for it, as they already do for `%%`. A host that wants comments
        published does it with a `beforeRender` extension, which keeps the
        escaping decision where it belongs.
      - THE SPELLING IS RECORDED. Both forms parse to a `comment` node, and the
        node says which one produced it (`delimited: true` and
        `form: "braced"` for this form, PART 12). Without that field a writer reading the tree back has one spelling
        for two constructs, and `foo {% bar %} baz` formats to `foo %% bar` --
        the trailing form swallowing `baz`.
      - `carve lint` reports a braced comment in a document that also carries
        template tags (`{% raw %}`, `{% endif %}`), which is the one shape
        where a Liquid or Nunjucks source reaches the parser as text.

   22. BRACED SPANS (the forced_* productions in PART 3). How a braced
      opener finds its closer, nests and fails is §9 R1; this section holds
      what §9 does not.
      - FORMS. Each emphasis mark has a braced form that renders the SAME
        element as its bare delimiter:
          {/x/}   -> <em>x</em>        {*x*}   -> <strong>x</strong>
          {_x_}   -> <u>x</u>          {~x~}   -> <s>x</s>
          {^x^}   -> <sup>x</sup>      {,x,}   -> <sub>x</sub>
          {=x=}   -> <mark>x</mark>
      - NO WORD BOUNDARY. A braced span opens and closes regardless of the
        surrounding characters, so it is the only way to emphasize
        intraword: foo{*bar*}baz -> foo<strong>bar</strong>baz. Its opener
        still needs a whole delimiter (CARVE-P3-025), and so do `{^`, `{,`,
        the editorial openers `{+`, `{-`, `{~` and `{#` and the comment `{%`.
      - DEPTH LIMIT. When an otherwise formed braced span would exceed the
        existing cap, its whole spelling, including its closer and nested
        markup, stays literal, and its closer MUST NOT close a parent. The
        literal range is bounded by the linear index of §9, not by parsing it
        again or rescanning the rest of the block for each rejected opener.
        The top inline run is level 1 of the cap, so 199 spans nest in it and
        the 200th stays literal.
        A bracket span counts toward the same cap: at most 199 formed spans of
        any mix nest in the top inline run.
        LITERAL here means the current inline source characters as text: do
        not decode escapes or produce nodes for code, autolinks or other
        markup inside the rejected range. Earlier document ingestion policies
        still apply. Bare and braced spans consume the same depth budget.
      - `{= … =}` is braced highlight (<mark>), distinct from the raw-inline
        `{=format}` attribute on a code span (`` `x`{=html} ``), which has no
        trailing `=` before the `}`.
      - ESCAPING. A literal `{/` (etc.) is written `\{/` -- one backslash on the
        brace is enough; the inner delimiter need not be escaped. That escapes
        the OPENER only: the inner delimiters stay active, so `\{*x*}` is a
        literal brace around a bare strong span. To keep a whole {*x*} literal,
        escape the active marks as well: \{\*x\*\}. The canonical writer
        applies ordinary literal escaping.
      - ATTRIBUTES. A braced span MAY carry a trailing `[attribute_run]` like
        any bare span: {*x*}{.c} -> <strong class="c">x</strong>. Child and
        parent attribute runs attach independently; neither inherits the
        other's attributes merely because they have the same kind.
      (corpus 01-emphasis-12 and -13;
      resources/spec/nested-braced-emphasis-cases.json.)

   23. LINE BLOCK (VERSE) -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-044] (governs `line_block` in PART 2). A
      `::: |` fenced block preserves the author's per-line layout.
      - TRIGGER. A bare pipe `|` TYPE TOKEN on the opener (`::: |`) selects this
        behavior. The token is a pipe, not a word. STRICT (djot): the opener
        carries no inline attributes, so the `::: {.line-block}` class form is an
        ordinary PARAGRAPH, not a line block -- the class alone does not trigger it.
      - WRAPPER. Renders as `<div class="line-block">` (a generic div, never an
        `<aside>`); extra classes / id attach via a PRECEDING block-attribute
        line, which floats onto the div (§15).
      - LINE BREAKS. Within a stanza, every `soft_break` node becomes a
        `hard_break` node. Consecutive non-blank lines render as one paragraph
        with `<br>` between them.

        AN EXISTING HARD BREAK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-045]. A `hard_break`,
        including one spelled with a backslash, is unchanged. It has already
        consumed its newline, so the stanza adds no second break.

        THE BACKSLASH STILL HAS A JOB HERE, so it is not redundant syntax the
        writer may drop. PART 7 makes the whitespace run before it INTERIOR,
        which is what lets a verse line keep a LONE trailing space that would
        otherwise be stripped from a content line; a run of TWO OR MORE
        trailing columns needs no backslash, because MEDIAL GAPS below has
        already turned it into NBSP CONTENT. And a `\` ALONE on a body line is
        how a stanza carries an EMPTY verse line, the blank line being the one
        spelling that would end the stanza instead. Both are observable in the
        tree and in the HTML, which is why the canonical writer may not drop
        either (CARVE-P11-020).

        AT ANY INLINE DEPTH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-046]. The conversion also
        applies inside emphasis, link text, spans and inline notes. A newline
        held in verbatim content is unchanged because it is not a `soft_break`
        node. The conversion is in the tree, not only in the HTML (PART 12).
      - STANZAS. A blank line ends a stanza; each stanza is its own `<p>` inside
        the single `<div class="line-block">`.
      - LEADING WHITESPACE. Each body line's leading whitespace is PRESERVED
        (unlike ordinary paragraph content, which is trimmed), down to a single
        column. In HTML each leading space serializes as a NBSP (`&nbsp;`,
        U+00A0), so indentation is visible with no external CSS (matching
        Pandoc). Plain-text / ANSI renderers emit the literal spaces. A tab in
        the leading run follows the tab-stop rule (PART 9 §24).
      - MEDIAL GAPS. An INNER or TRAILING run of TWO OR MORE columns is
        preserved the same way and serializes as the same NBSPs. This is the
        inline alignment verse and address blocks are made of -- the caesura of
        Old English verse, a column of aligned text -- and collapsing it loses
        the per-line layout the block exists to keep (matching Pandoc, where
        every space the author writes is significant). A LONE inner space is NOT
        preserved: it stays an ordinary, collapsible space so a long line can
        still wrap between words. Tab arithmetic is the leading run's, counted
        from the column the run starts at -- so the SAME tab is a gap or a lone
        space depending on the column it lands in, which is what makes it worth
        pinning: corpus 41-line-blocks-9 holds a tab that crosses one column
        (collapses) beside two that cross two stops (kept), and a leading one.
      - REFERENCE COLUMN. Leading whitespace is measured RELATIVE TO THE FENCE
        indent, not absolute column 0: a `line-block` nested in a list has the
        container's structural indent stripped first (the list dedents to its
        content column), so only the author's intra-verse indent survives. The
        `+` first-block form (fence at the marker column) is the canonical way to nest a
        `line-block` without the container consuming indentation.
      - INLINE CONTENT parses normally (emphasis, links, code, ...); only the
        whitespace and soft-break handling differ from an ordinary div.
      - NO BLOCK CONSTRUCT IS RECOGNIZED INSIDE ONE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-047]. A body line
        is verse: a link, footnote or abbreviation definition, a
        block-attribute line and a fence opener are all ORDINARY TEXT there,
        and none of them is REGISTERED. The stanza is the only structure a
        line block has.

        A COMMENT IS THE ONE EXCEPTION, and the exception is about TIMING
        rather than about what a comment is. `comment_line` IS a block, listed
        in PART 1 among the INVISIBLE blocks and ruled by §10 I5 to interrupt
        an open paragraph and be consumed. It survives the suppression above
        because it is the only construct on that list whose recognition
        PRESERVES the verse instead of rewriting it: a definition or a fence
        opener CLAIMS the line and changes what renders, while a comment-only
        body line leaves an EMPTY verse line.

        `comment_block` IS THE SECOND EXCEPTION, because hiding text is what
        the construct is for. A `%%%` fence line indented by at most 3 spaces
        opens a block comment in verse under §28's rules, as an indented
        opener does everywhere else (carve#3885 rule A, carve#4469): the
        closer matches on exact delimiter length, its leading whitespace is
        not verse and is allowed, and the tails are ignored. The whole span
        leaves NOTHING: no verse line, no line break, and a blank line in its
        body ends no stanza. A verse block holding only such a span renders
        with no stanza, so `::: |` / `  %%%` / `  secret` / `  %%%` / `:::`
        renders an empty line block with the blank line required between its
        opening and closing tags by CARVE-P10-001. A fence with no closer in
        the body degrades to a `comment_line` (§28) and leaves an empty verse
        line like any other; an indented one is verse text, as an indented
        `%%` line is below.
        The span is a `comment` node in the tree, so the canonical writer emits
        it back unchanged.

        AN INVISIBLE LINE IS REMOVED AT THE BLOCK LAYER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-048]. The line
        is decided
        with the other block-layer decisions, BEFORE any inline content
        exists. No inline run can reach it, so whether an EARLIER line left a
        verbatim run open cannot change whether a line is a comment. Otherwise
        §21's verbatim exclusion claims the line and PUBLISHES the comment's
        own text -- the one outcome a comment may never have -- on a document
        whose only defect is a stray backtick above it:

          ::: |
          a `b
          %% secret
          c
          :::

        The comment is gone before the inline run exists, and the run carries
        the empty verse line as a NEWLINE, like every other break it swallows.

        REMOVED FROM THE RENDER, NOT FROM THE TREE. The line is a `comment`
        node like any other comment (CARVE-P9-042, PART 12), so the canonical writer
        emits it back UNCHANGED and at the SAME column. A writer that indents
        it by a space to stop the line re-reading as a comment publishes the
        very text the removal exists to hide, because in verse a leading run
        is CONTENT (below) and an indented `%%` line is ordinary text: a
        PART 11 §1 failure.

        THE NODE'S SURVIVAL DOES NOT DEPEND ON HOW THE BOUNDARY ABOVE IT WAS
        SPELLED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-049]. `a` over `%% c` and `a \` over `%% c` are the
        same document but for a backslash on the line above, and the comment
        is a node in both. The boundary that OPENS the comment's line is there
        either way, which is the only thing the line needs to exist.

        BUT IT DOES NOT SURVIVE A RUN THAT ATE ITS LINE. What an unclosed
        verbatim run carries across an emptied line is the NEWLINE, so there
        is no boundary left in the tree for a `comment` node to sit on and the
        run's value holds an EMPTY LINE instead. Appending the node anyway puts its
        span before the run that contains it and after the node that follows
        it, which PART 12 containment refuses. The writer's spelling for that
        empty line, and for every emptied line the run still spans, is
        CARVE-P11-020.

        A TRAILING COMMENT IS A DIFFERENT CONSTRUCT and this clause does not
        reach it. `x %% secret` is `inline_comment` (PART 3, CARVE-P9-041), not
        `comment_line`, and CARVE-P9-041's verbatim rule governs it: inside a verbatim
        run there is no comment there at all, only two percent characters in
        content. The asymmetry is deliberate: an engine may leave a `%%`
        standing inside a verbatim run, and may never delete author bytes out
        of one.

        LEADING WHITESPACE IS CONTENT HERE, so `comment_line`'s optional
        `[whitespace]` prefix has nothing to consume in verse: ONLY a body
        line whose FIRST character is `%` is a comment line. An indented `%%`
        line is ordinary verse text and its leading run serializes as NBSPs
        like any other.

        AN EMPTY VERSE LINE IS A LINE, NOT A BREAK. It contributes a `<br>`
        only through the BOUNDARY above it, exactly as a line with content
        does, so a comment-only line at the END of a stanza adds nothing after
        the break that already separates it from the line before: `a` over
        `%% c` is `a<br>` and a stanza that is nothing but a comment is an
        EMPTY paragraph. Anything else would put two breaks on one boundary,
        which is what CARVE-P9-045 refuses above.

      (corpus 41-line-blocks*.)

      LOCAL HARD-BREAK BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-050] (governs `local_hard_break_block` in
      PART 2). A `::: \` fenced block is the smaller local opt-in for visible
      line breaks.
      - TRIGGER. A bare backslash `\` TYPE TOKEN on the opener (`::: \`) selects
        this behavior. The token is not an English type word. Extra classes / id
        attach via a PRECEDING block-attribute line.
      - WRAPPER. Renders as `<div class="hardbreaks">` (a generic div, never an
        `<aside>`).
      - DIRECT PARAGRAPHS ONLY. Each soft break in a direct paragraph child of
        the block becomes a hard break (`<br>`). Nested blocks parse normally and
        keep normal soft-break behavior; the mode is not inherited.
      - NO WHITESPACE PRESERVATION. Unlike `::: |`, leading whitespace has no
        special visual-preservation rule. Ordinary paragraph parsing trims or
        folds it as usual.

      (corpus 41-line-blocks*.)

   24. TABS AND INDENTATION -- FORMALIZED COLUMN ARITHMETIC (governs
      `indent` in PART 2 and every indentation-sensitive comparison; the
      arithmetic PART 0's layout automaton runs on).
      C1 COLUMNS, NOT CHARACTERS. col advances by 1 for a space and to the
         next multiple of 4 for a tab (CommonMark tab stops; a leading tab
         indents to column 4). Outside indentation (code content, inline
         text) tabs are PRESERVED verbatim and never expanded (PART 2,
         TABS IN CODE).
      C2 MARKER SEPARATOR IS A SPACE. The required separator after a list
         marker is a single SPACE character -- a syntax delimiter, not
         indentation. `-<TAB>a` is NOT a list item (paragraph text).
      C3 MARKER-BODY COLUMN AND EXTENT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-083]. A list
         item, a definition body and a footnote definition body use this one
         rule set. The content column is the visual column where the marker's
         body starts. For a list item it is marker width plus separator
         (`- ` -> 2,
         `1. ` -> 3, `10. ` -> 4). A marker-attached attribute block (§15 A8)
         is metadata and contributes ZERO: `-{.x} ` -> 2 and `1.{.x} ` -> 3,
         irrespective of the block's spelling, length or Unicode encoding. A
         task checkbox is content and contributes zero for the same coordinate:
         `-{.x} [x] ` -> 2. A definition body's own `: ` separator sets its
         content column, including every extra separator space. A footnote
         body adds two columns to its marker's authored column and consumes
         exactly two columns of prefix. All comparisons use C1; no marker
         family has its own reach test.

         A nonblank line at or past the content column belongs to the body.
         A recognized block opener there begins a child block. A blank run of
         any length remains in the body when the next nonblank line reaches
         that column. A blank run without such a follower ends the body before
         the next line. The list-only three-blank sibling boundary in §11 N1a
         decides list grouping, not body ownership. A line below the column
         follows PART 0 S4 and S5 (CARVE-P0-024): it may continue the deepest
         open paragraph lazily when §10 permits, and otherwise closes the body
         and is read in the nearest surviving owner. This applies to footnotes
         as it does to list items, including flush-left ordinary text without
         a blank. A lone `+` at the live marker column, including the opener's
         sole first-block content, uses §17 L3 to attach one
         block; refusal keeps the marker as a letter. These decisions are
         independent of the marker spelling and the rendered element.

         A child -- a LIST MARKER (bullet `-`/`*`,
         task, or ordered, SYMMETRIC) opening a sublist, OR any OTHER block
         opener -- must reach AT LEAST the parent item's content_column to belong
         to the item. The block-opener set is UNIFORM and closed: block quote (`>` or
         `> `), heading (`#`), thematic break, fenced code, colon fence / admonition
         (`:::`), TABLE (a `|`-delimited row), and DEFINITION LIST (a `:: term`
         opener; the two-line `:: `/`: ` marker is recognized by the same
         look-ahead the top level uses, and only the `:: ` TERM line opens the
         block). None of these is special-cased: a def-list or table interrupts
         at column 0 and nests at content_column exactly as a quote or heading
         does. A definition term is a leaf with no ownership of its own (PART 0
         FAMILIES). The content_column is
         the item body's CANONICAL COLUMN 0 and its minimum containment column.
         This one rule holds whether or not a blank line precedes the child;
         the blank only decides tight vs loose (§17), and there is no relaxed
         `base_column + 2` channel.
           - BELOW content_column: the line is outside the item body and
             never nests. It folds into the deepest open paragraph when its
             form does not interrupt (PART 0 S4), and otherwise ends the item
             and is read in the nearest surviving owner (S5). That owner is the
             document only when no enclosing container's content column is
             reached.

             THE FOLD PRESUMES AN OPEN PARAGRAPH -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-051].
             A fold continues an open paragraph. Where none is open the line
             is classified in the owner that SURVIVES: after a blank line the
             item has ended, and a comment below the content column closes the
             item like any other interrupter (PART 0 S1, S5). It holds for
             bullet, ordered, task and attributed markers alike.

             AN INVISIBLE LINE BELOW THE COLUMN INTERRUPTS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-052].
             Below the column an invisible line keeps its class (§10 I5): a
             comment, link or footnote definition or block-attribute line
             interrupts, closes the item and is read in the surviving owner,
             where a definition registers (CARVE-P0-010) and an attribute line
             is pending (§15 A2). An abbreviation definition below document
             level is text (CARVE-P12-026).
           - AT OR PAST content_column: a block opener nests and a list marker
             opens a sublist. A non-list opener past the column opens in the
             item, measured from its own line (PART 0 S3); a sublist marker
             keeps its at-or-past containment rule.
             A processor with lint diagnostics SHOULD report this ambiguous
             compatibility spelling and name both author choices: dedent to
             content_column for the canonical structural spelling, or escape
             the opener to preserve the literal reading. It SHOULD likewise
             report a block-shaped line between the marker and content_column
             that detached after a blank. The stable rule ids and exact scopes
             are `list-item-block-overindented` and `list-item-body-detached`
             in docs/validation.md. The default formatter MUST NOT choose one
             intention for the author while migrating an older spelling.
         This is intentionally SIMILAR TO DJOT'S LENIENCY inside list items:
         any authored column at or beyond Carve's minimum content_column may
         open the block (docs/divergence-from-djot.md).
      C4 RULE B (TOP LEVEL, ANY INDENT). A bullet (`-`/`*` + space +
         content) opens a list at ANY indentation at the TOP LEVEL -- no
         column-0 restriction (unlike CommonMark, `    - x` is a list, not
         indented code); an ordered marker behaves the same. Scope: WHERE a
         top-level list may open -- NOT sublist nesting (C3) and NOT
         interrupting an open paragraph (§10 I2).
      C5 DEDENT. Re-parsing nested content strips the container's
         structural indent BY COLUMNS. A tab straddling the dedent boundary
         is consumed by columns on every line: its unconsumed columns are
         re-inserted as spaces. This keeps mixed tab/space list markers
         aligned, gives a tab-indented code or `%%%` comment payload line (§28)
         the indentation its space spelling has, and holds beyond the host
         column. A LIST-MARKER
         line is a bullet or ordered marker line and equally a definition-list
         `:: ` term or `: ` description marker line.

   25. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-054] (HTML output target). These rules
      bind any implementation that emits HTML for UNTRUSTED input, so an
      implementation written to this grammar + the corpus cannot be conformant
      yet expose a trivial XSS vector. The corpus pins representative cases
      (resources/examples/edge-cases/ "Security hardening").
      - URL SINK SCHEME DENYLIST. On every clickable URL sink -- link
        destination (`href`), image source (`src`), and autolink -- an HTML
        renderer MUST reject any URL whose scheme is `javascript`, `vbscript`,
        `data`, or `file`, emitting an EMPTY value (`href=""` / `src=""`).
        The denylist additionally covers the OS protocol-handler and
        command-execution class (CVE-2026-20841 class): `ms-msdt`, `ms-office`,
        `ms-word`, `ms-excel`, `ms-powerpoint`, `ms-access`, `ms-visio`,
        `ms-project`, `ms-publisher`, `ms-infopath`, `ms-spd`, `ms-search`,
        `search-ms`, `ms-cxh`, `ms-cxh-full`, `shell`, `vscode`,
        `vscode-insiders`, and `jar`. These route to an operating-system
        handler that can launch a binary or open a macro-bearing document, so
        they are blanked like `javascript:`. Ordinary web and contact schemes
        -- `http`, `https`, `mailto`, `tel`, `ftp`, `sms` -- remain allowed.
        Scheme detection MUST first strip C0 and C1 control characters and DEL
        anywhere in the value (`java` + U+0001 + `script:` is `javascript:`)
        AND ALL UNICODE WHITESPACE -- not just ASCII space/tab/newline but
        every Unicode space separator, including NEXT LINE (U+0085), NARROW
        NO-BREAK SPACE (U+202F), NBSP (U+00A0), the U+2000..U+200A spaces, MEDIUM
        MATHEMATICAL SPACE (U+205F), IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE (U+3000), the
        line/paragraph separators (U+2028 / U+2029), and the BOM (U+FEFF) --
        before matching the scheme, so an obfuscated `<U+202F>javascript:`
        destination cannot slip past the denylist. A link
        destination cannot hold such a character (PART 3), so only an attribute
        value reaches this strip; the corpus row "Scheme probe strips Unicode
        whitespace" pins that a reference definition cannot hand one over.

        The probe does not strip the wider `Cf` category.
        A `data:` exception for images, if offered, MUST be opt-in. An
        authored `href` on a link or `src` on an image is ignored and the
        destination stands (`[a](/u){href=/v}` keeps `/u`), so an attribute
        block cannot reintroduce a rejected scheme.

        THIS APPLIES TO EVERY TARGET THAT EMITS A RESOLVABLE URL, not only to
        the HTML renderer. A Markdown target's link and image destinations are
        resolved by whatever renders that Markdown, so a scheme blanked here
        and passed through there is not blocked -- it is deferred by one step.
        A target that emits no URL, such as plain text, has nothing to blank
        and is unaffected. THE ONE URL PLAIN TEXT EMITS is the destination of
        an unused link reference definition it writes (CARVE-P11-028), so that
        line is bound: a denied destination there is blanked, `[y]:`, and takes
        a `destination-denied` row. Corpus 108-security-hardening-12 pins it.

        "EMITS A RESOLVABLE URL" IS ABOUT THE URL REACHING A READER THAT
        RESOLVES IT, not about the target emitting a hyperlink construct. The
        TERMINAL target is bound: it prints a link's destination beside the
        text, and every current terminal emulator autolinks a URL in its output
        and hands the scheme to the OS handler when the reader clicks it.

        CANONICAL CARVE SOURCE IS AN INTERCHANGE FORM, NOT A URL SINK. As an
        explicit exception to the rule above, the PART 11 writer (`carve fmt`
        and the `carve` target) MUST preserve authored destinations, including
        denied schemes, to satisfy its parse round-trip invariant. It does not
        apply this denylist and owes no `destination-denied` row for a
        destination it preserves. The AST exit preserves the same values
        (CARVE-P12-008). A consumer that renders either interchange form MUST
        apply this clause at its own URL sinks (CARVE-P12-009); formatting or
        serializing source is not sanitization.
        Markdown is a lossy export for readers that this specification does
        not bind, so its destinations remain subject to the denylist.

        WHAT IS BLANKED IS THE DESTINATION, NOT THE TEXT. A denied autolink's
        visible text IS its URL, and every target shows it. Blanking there
        would edit what the author wrote rather than withhold a destination:
        not required.

        A BLANKED DESTINATION OWES ONE ROW on the `CARVE-P2-024` render-loss
        report under the code `destination-denied`, in BOTH safe modes: the
        flag does not gate the hardening, so it does not gate the reporting
        either. A link or autolink destination and an image source blank the
        same way and each take one row. The emitted value does not change;
        a checked render reports what it blanked.
      - ATTRIBUTE NAME/VALUE HARDENING. On EVERY rendered element -- including
        elements emitted by extensions -- an HTML renderer MUST drop
        attributes whose name begins with `on` (case-insensitive) and the
        names `srcdoc` and `formaction`; MUST blank any AUTHORED attribute
        value whose scheme, probed as above (control characters and Unicode
        whitespace stripped), is on the URL SINK SCHEME DENYLIST above: the
        four schemes and the OS protocol-handler class alike, so
        `{title="Shell: bash"}` renders `title=""`; and MUST blank any `style`
        value containing `expression(`, `url(`, `@import`, `behavior:`, or
        `-moz-binding` (all whitespace removed first, so `ex pression(`
        matches, and ASCII-case-insensitively, so `URL(` does). An authored value is one written in an attribute block or
        one an extension copies onto its wrapper element: an extension MUST
        route authored attributes through the same hardening and MUST NOT
        emit them raw. `id` and the id slot are not URL sinks and are not
        probed: `{id="javascript:x"}` executes nothing, and blanking it would
        break anchors and cross-references to an explicit id. A value the renderer derives from text is not an
        authored value and is not blanked: a link title (`[a](u "Shell: x")`
        keeps `title="Shell: x"`), an image's alt text, and a task box's
        accessible name stay as written.
        EVERY PROBE IN THIS CLAUSE READS THE VALUE AS IT WILL BE EMITTED. An
        authored attribute value keeps a bidi override or isolate control, as a
        destination does: `{style="ur` + U+202E + `l(x)"}` renders that value
        unchanged, where it is no `url(`. A renderer that removes a character
        from an authored value MUST remove it before probing.

        A URL-LIST ATTRIBUTE IS PROBED TOKEN-WISE, NOT AT ITS HEAD --
        NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-055]. Four attributes carry a LIST of URLs: `srcset`,
        `imagesrcset`, `ping` and `attributionsrc`. For those four names,
        matched case-insensitively, a renderer MUST split the value into
        tokens, apply to every non-empty token THE SAME PROBE this clause
        applies to a value as a whole, and blank the ENTIRE value when ANY
        token matches or when the whole value does. The `srcset` value
        `safe.png 1x,javascript:alert(1) 2x` is blanked.

        - THE SEPARATORS are the attribute's own: `srcset` and `imagesrcset`
          split on commas and ASCII whitespace, `ping` and `attributionsrc`
          on ASCII whitespace only. A srcset URL that itself contains a comma
          is split too, so `https://example.com/a,data:x 1x` is blanked.
        - THE STRIP IS PER TOKEN: each token is control-stripped and
          Unicode-whitespace-stripped before its scheme is read. The strip
          creates no boundary, so `a<U+202F>javascript:x` is one token and
          is kept. A token beginning with a `Cf` character is left alone at
          every position, as at the head of a single URL.
        - THE VALUE-WIDE PROBE STILL RUNS, and it strips the ASCII whitespace
          the split breaks on: `ping="java script:alert(1)"` is blanked.
        - WHAT IS BLANKED IS THE WHOLE VALUE, never one candidate.
        - THE SET IS CLOSED BY A CRITERION: an attribute whose value grammar
          is a LIST of URLs that a consumer RESOLVES OR FETCHES. `itemtype`,
          `itemprop`, `itemid`, `archive`, `for` and `headers` are outside
          it, and `style` has its own rule above. PROSE ATTRIBUTES (`title`,
          `alt`, `aria-label`) MUST NOT be tokenized.
      - RAW PASSTHROUGH OPT-OUT. Raw blocks (```=FORMAT, PART 2) and raw
        inline (`{=format}`, §20) emit verbatim UNESCAPED content. An
        implementation MUST provide a mode in which raw passthrough is NOT
        emitted and is instead escaped as literal text. Implementations SHOULD
        make safe handling the default for untrusted input; a processor that
        emits raw passthrough by default MUST document that its input is
        trusted.
      - URL TEMPLATE ENCODING. Any value taken from document content (mention
        name, tag, citation key, crossref id) and substituted into a
        configured URL template MUST be percent-encoded before substitution,
        and the resulting URL MUST then pass the scheme denylist above.
      - MATH + CODE are element TEXT, not raw HTML: their verbatim content MUST
        be HTML-escaped (`&`,`<`,`>`) before wrapping (§18, PART 10 §6). They
        are NOT a raw-HTML bypass.
      - FRONTMATTER MUST be parsed with a SAFE loader -- no arbitrary object
        instantiation and no custom-tag deserialization (e.g. no YAML
        `!!`-tag object construction). Any frontmatter value later rendered
        into output MUST be escaped per the output target's rules.
      - RESOURCE BOUNDS (DoS). An implementation MUST parse and render any
        input in time and space LINEAR in input size (within a constant
        factor). It MUST bound abbreviation, reference, footnote, and crossref
        expansion so total work stays O(n): reject an empty abbreviation term,
        avoid per-occurrence rescanning, and bound target cloning. It MUST enforce a FINITE nesting-depth cap for block and
        inline containers, beyond which further openers degrade to literal
        text rather than recursing. The cap is a single constant
        MAX_NESTING_DEPTH applied UNIFORMLY to blockquote, list, and
        fenced-div / admonition nesting (and to footnote-body and the inline
        recursion): the reference uses MAX_NESTING_DEPTH = 200, and a
        conformant implementation SHOULD use the same value (it MAY choose a
        larger fixed cap, but never an unbounded one). Past the cap every
        container kind FLATTENS the same way -- the opener becomes literal
        paragraph text -- rather than crashing.

        A FLATTENED OPENER IS ORDINARY PARAGRAPH TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-056], and
        therefore groups by the ordinary paragraph rule: consecutive
        over-cap openers, and any text following them, form ONE paragraph,
        ending at the first blank line like any other. There is no trailing
        newline before `</p>`. With MAX_NESTING_DEPTH = 200, 203 `::: note`
        lines followed by `x` end:

          <p>::: note
          ::: note
          ::: note
          x</p>

        "Degrades to literal text" is the whole rule; nothing about being
        past the cap makes a line group differently from the same characters
        typed by an author.

        A RETURNED DOCUMENT NEVER SILENTLY LACKS AUTHORED TEXT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-068].
        Every bound here, or added by an implementation (including work,
        diagnostic and probe budgets), has two permitted outcomes:
        - DEGRADE while preserving every authored character in the output;
          structure, symbol-table entries or report rows may be lost. The
          nesting cap above preserves an over-cap opener as literal text;
          an unresolved reference likewise renders its literal source.
        - REFUSE the whole operation with a typed failure, as the render
          ceiling below requires.
        A returned document MUST NOT lack text the author typed. An unresolved
        footnote reference's attribute run is consumed (PART 9R R2), which is
        that construct's rule and not such a bound.

        THE CANONICAL WRITER MAY TAKE THE REFUSE ARM PAST THE CAP -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-078]. Over-cap flattening owes it no spellable tree: the
        flattened opener is literal paragraph text carrying the ladder's own
        indentation, and a value line that starts with whitespace has no Carve
        spelling. The writer's typed refusal is therefore a legitimate outcome
        of the cap and not a defect. What a writer MUST NOT do is the third
        thing, which is neither arm of the rule above: emit source that does
        not read back as the document it was given. Every other target still
        renders the degraded tree.

        Recursive RENDER / RESOLVE / FILTER passes over a programmatically
        constructed tree MUST be bounded too, and their ceiling MUST NOT be
        reachable by a tree the SAME implementation's parser produces: it
        MUST EXCEED MAX_NESTING_DEPTH by enough to cover the blocks a
        container subtree adds. An implementation MUST DERIVE that margin
        from the worst per-level cost of ITS OWN depth unit, as PART 12
        §9(c) requires of the ingest bound, and MUST NOT adopt another
        implementation's number: a list level costs one container-depth step
        and two AST node levels. A multiple of MAX_NESTING_DEPTH and an
        offset added to it both conform. The reference counts container
        depth and uses MAX_NESTING_DEPTH + 32 = 232.

        AT THE RENDER CEILING, A RENDERER REFUSES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-057]. Reaching it
        MUST produce a typed, documented failure naming the depth bound, never
        silent truncation, a partial document or the host's stack-overflow error.
        This applies PART 12 §9(b)'s ingest rule to rendering, including the
        canonical writer. The derived ceiling exceeds the parser's bound in
        its own unit, so parsed trees cannot reach it; CARVE-P12-028(b) already rejects
        ingested trees deeper than the parser's limit. Trees built through the
        API can reach it and MUST receive that failure.

      - FILE INCLUSION. The `{{ … }}` directive (CARVE-P6-001) does no I/O in the
        parser and stays literal until a host configures a resolver (PART 6 I3). Enabling
        it for UNTRUSTED input requires one explicit, absolute containment root
        that never defaults to the process working directory, containment
        decided on the canonical path after symlink resolution, no remote fetch
        without an explicit allowlist, and the cycle, depth, size and call
        bounds (PART 6 I6, I10). Included content receives the same sanitization as
        its parent.
        Inclusion MUST remain disabled on server-side rendering paths for
        user-supplied content. If such a host offers inclusion, it MUST require
        an explicit, administrator-only opt-in with an explicit root; that
        opt-in MUST NOT be inherited by front-end rendering paths.
      - NON-HTML TARGETS. The Markdown, plain-text, and terminal (ANSI)
        renderers MUST NOT become injection vectors either. The TERMINAL
        target MUST strip text, code, math AND URL values of every control
        character before emission -- the non-whitespace C0 controls, DEL
        (U+007F) and the C1 controls alike, the last because CSI (U+009B)
        and OSC (U+009D) are single-character forms of the very sequences
        this bullet exists to stop. A Markdown link/image title MUST escape
        the `"` that would otherwise terminate it. WHICH TARGET STRIPS A C0
        CONTROL is §29, not this bullet. The narrowing is per TARGET and applies to C0 alone; it
        never narrows what the terminal target strips.

   ============================================================================ *)
